<h2>Separating the Perils in a Storm Claim</h2>
<p>Insurance policies do not cover &ldquo;storms&rdquo; as such. They cover named perils, and each one has its own rules. Sorting damage by cause is often the most important part of a storm claim, because it decides which deductible, limit, and exclusion applies to each item on the estimate.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail.</strong> Roofs, windows, siding, gutters, and AC units. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">San Antonio hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Wind.</strong> Missing shingles, displaced metal panels, fences, carports, and fallen trees. See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Rain through a storm-created opening.</strong> Often covered as part of the wind or hail loss.</li>
<li><strong>Rising or surface water.</strong> Generally excluded under homeowners and commercial policies; requires flood insurance.</li>
<li><strong>Lightning and power surges.</strong> Fire, electrical damage, and damaged equipment, depending on policy terms.</li>
<li><strong>Ice and freezing.</strong> Weight of ice on roofs and limbs, and burst pipes. See <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>When an insurer pays one peril and denies another, the claim is not necessarily wrong, but the reasoning should be explained in writing and tied to policy language.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Do-I-Need-A-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Couple meeting with an adjuster holding a clipboard outside a home with a blue tarp on the roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>After a storm, walking the property together helps identify every area of damage before the insurer's inspection.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Documenting a Combined Storm Loss</h2>
<p>A storm claim gets stronger when the evidence is organized by area and by cause:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Exterior first.</strong> Photograph every roof slope, elevation, window, fence line, and outbuilding.</li>
<li><strong>Interior second.</strong> Photograph ceiling stains, wet drywall, and flooring, and note which roof area sits above each.</li>
<li><strong>Weather data.</strong> Save NOAA and National Weather Service reports for the date, including hail size and wind speeds near the property.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigation records.</strong> Keep tarp invoices, drying logs, and moisture readings.</li>
<li><strong>Contents.</strong> List damaged personal property with age and approximate cost.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers each step in more detail.</p>

