<h2>Tropical Systems and Heavy Rain</h2>
<p>San Antonio sits well inland, but tropical systems still reach it. When Hurricane Harvey moved inland in late August 2017, NOAA logged a tropical storm event for the Bexar County zone (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=720275" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). Tropical systems that weaken over land can still bring gusty winds and days of heavy rain to the San Antonio area. Tropical rain is where storm claims and flood claims most often collide: water that comes through a damaged roof is treated very differently from water that rises from the ground. If both happened, document them separately and expect two different coverage discussions, one under your property policy and one under any flood policy you carry.</p>

<h2>Be Careful With Door-Knockers After a Storm</h2>
<p>After a large hail or wind event, out-of-town contractors and solicitors often arrive within days. Some are reputable; some are not. Before you sign anything, read it. A contract that assigns your claim rights, or that lets a contractor negotiate your claim, deserves close review. In Texas, only a licensed public adjuster (or an attorney) may represent you in adjusting your claim for a fee, and a public adjuster cannot also be your repair contractor on the same claim. You can verify a public adjuster's license with the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>.</p>

<h2>Policy Terms That Matter After a Storm</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductible.</strong> Many Texas policies apply a percentage deductible to wind and hail losses. Bexar County is not in the TWIA area, so this deductible comes from your regular homeowners or commercial policy.</li>
<li><strong>Replacement cost vs. actual cash value.</strong> Insurers often pay ACV first and release depreciation after repairs. Some roof coverage is ACV-only.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> Code-required upgrades during repairs are paid only up to this coverage's limit, if you have it.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of use / business income.</strong> If the property cannot be used during repairs, these coverages may help.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Insurer Timelines in Texas</h2>
<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets prompt-payment deadlines. An insurer generally has 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim, begin its investigation, and request information, then 15 business days after receiving what it asked for to accept or reject the claim (or explain why it needs more time, up to 45 more days). After a weather-related catastrophe declared by the Texas Insurance Commissioner, these deadlines are extended by 15 days. If your claim has stalled, see <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How San Antonio Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster works for the policyholder. After a storm, we may inspect the full property, separate the damage by peril, prepare a line-item estimate, assemble the weather evidence, and handle communication with the insurer's adjuster. If the claim was already paid, we can review the estimate for missed items. We serve property owners throughout San Antonio, Bexar County, and surrounding communities, and we can step in before the first inspection or after a payment that did not cover the repairs. We cannot promise a particular result; coverage depends on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
